\newcommand{\Hint}{\mathcal{H}}
\newcommand{\Z}{\mathbb{Z}}
\newcommand{\Q}{\mathbb{Q}}

\title{The Hint Value Has No Size Law:\\
Uniform-Fibre Transport, Residue Lifts, and Exact Plateaus\\
for Semiprime Label Channels}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{October 3, 2026}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
Let $N=pq$ be a semiprime, $m$ a modulus, and $L$ a \emph{label}: an arithmetic invariant of the unordered pair $\{p,q\}$, such as the pair of root counts of a fixed polynomial modulo $p$ and modulo $q$. The \emph{hint value}
\[
\Hint = I\bigl(L;(p \bmod m,\ q\bmod m)\bigr) - I\bigl(L;\ N\bmod m\bigr)
\]
measures how much a factor-level residue clue is worth beyond what the product already reveals. An experiment on four batteries ($S_3$ at conductor $31$, $C_3$ at $7$, $D_4$ at $8$, $C_5$ at $11$) found $\Hint$ flat to within $5.3\%$ across factor sizes $k=14,18,22$, a $16{,}384$-fold span. There were two complications: the $S_3$ battery read anomalously high at $k=10$, and a ``which-factor wall'' held only for a conditional test statistic. We prove that each observation is structural.
\begin{enumerate}
\item Mutual information is invariant under uniform-fibre maps with matching pointwise count ratios. Hence replicating residue classes by any number of prime identities leaves $\Hint$ exactly unchanged.
\item (Residue lift.) For any finite abelian conductor group with a surjective dial, $\Hint$ equals its value on a small dial box, independently of size and conductor.
\item The exact plateaus are $\tfrac12$ ($S_3$), $\log_2 3-\tfrac23$ ($C_3$), $\tfrac32-\tfrac9{32}\log_23$ ($D_4$) and $\log_25-\tfrac{12}{25}\log_23-\tfrac{16}{25}$ ($C_5$), with residuals $\log_23-\tfrac79$, $\tfrac{15}{32}$, $0$, $0$.
\item $\Hint=H(L\mid N)-H(L\mid P)$. Thin pools can only inflate $\Hint$, never beyond $H(L\mid N)$.
\item For swap-invariant populations, $I(O;V)=0$ for every orientation bit $O$ and symmetric view $V$, while an unconditional statistic can read a full bit.
\end{enumerate}
The reported $S_3$ plateau $\approx0.54$ is the exact value $\tfrac12$ plus plug-in bias of $\approx0.040$ bits.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}

\subsection{The question}
Factoring $N=pq$ is believed hard, yet much about $p$ and $q$ is visible through $N$. For instance, $N\bmod m=(p\bmod m)(q\bmod m)\bmod m$ constrains the residues of the factors. A natural research programme asks which information about the factors is released by the product and which is walled off. A quantitative instrument for this question is the hint value. It compares the factor-pair residue view $P=(p\bmod m,q\bmod m)$, which is not available from $N$, with the product view $N\bmod m$, which is. Every practical measurement is made at modest factor sizes. To extrapolate, one must know how $\Hint$ depends on size. We show that, in the natural population model, it does not depend on size at all.

\subsection{The experiment}
For each battery, $n=15{,}000$ semiprimes were sampled per factor size, and $\Hint$ was estimated by plug-in entropies (Table~\ref{tab:exp}).

