\section{Statistics}
Since the population value is exact, the residual deviation is estimator bias. For $S_3$ at $m=31$ ($\varphi=30$), the joint supports are $|J_P|=2\varphi^2=1800$ and $|J_N|=3\varphi=90$, and the view supports are $|V_P|=900$ and $|V_N|=30$. The first-order bias is
\[
\frac{(|J_P|-|V_P|)-(|J_N|-|V_N|)}{2n\ln2}=\frac{840}{2\cdot15000\cdot\ln2}\approx0.0404,
\]
which predicts $\approx0.540$ against the reported $0.5425/0.5415$. Seeded simulations of the ideal law give $0.544$--$0.553$.

\section{Algorithms}
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(A)] \emph{Exact hint.} Tabulate the counts of $L,P,N,(L,P),(L,N)$ and return $[H(L,N)-H(N)]-[H(L,P)-H(P)]$. This takes $O(|\Omega|)$ time.
\item[(B)] \emph{Residue lift.} Enumerate the dial box ($|H|^2|C|$ points) instead of the residue space ($|G|^2|C|$ points). For $S_3$ this means $36$ points instead of $8100$, at any conductor.
\item[(C)] \emph{Conditional wall test.} Group the sample by the symmetric view, and calibrate against orientation flips within groups, which is the exact null by Theorem~\ref{thm:wall}.
\end{enumerate}

\section{Discussion}
Sections~2--6 are exact statements about finite populations. The identification of real batteries with the residue-space population is the Dirichlet--Chebotarev model, which is an assumption about the distribution of primes. Hint values transfer across sizes wherever the pool resolves the conductor's classes; the observed floor was about $30$ primes per class. Where the pool is thin, the error is upward and bounded by the residual. The which-factor wall holds exactly, provided it is tested conditionally.

\section{Future directions}
\begin{enumerate}
\item \emph{Plug-in bias law:} the expected $S_3$ reading is $\tfrac12+\frac{(|J_P|-|V_P|)-(|J_N|-|V_N|)}{2n\ln2}+O(n^{-2})$, predicting $0.5404\pm0.004$.
\item \emph{Dihedral universality:} for $D_n$ with quadratic-resolvent dial and root-count labels, the hint value is $\tfrac12$ for odd $n$ and an explicit combination of $\log_23$ and $\log_25$ for even $n$.
\item \emph{Pool-resolution threshold:} with $r$ primes per class the expected hint value is $\tfrac12+H(L\mid P)\,\mathbb E[\mathrm{leak}(r)]$ with $\mathrm{leak}(r)=\Theta(1/r)$.
\end{enumerate}

